Z||000011||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||02-25-2001||03:07 AM||||Oreif,<P>I am looking at the pictures you posted and it appears that your scoop does not connect to the enine's intake.  I can see the pretty red intake manifold, but I am "baffled" as to how the scoop can be of any use.<P>I have a 4cylinder and I think the scoop ends up right over the air cleaner assembly.  So, it seems like a true ram air with the indy style snorkle would be ideal for a duke/super duty engine.<P>Duh, as they would say, but your pictures are the first I have seen detailing what's under the pretty snorkle.<P>For now, I like using my little trunk to carry books and small parts from the local Kragen's.  Removing four bolts all the time would diminish the fun for me.<P>What if the snorkle were attached to the engine and the decklid cut out to allow it to stay put?  Could you make a set up that would allow the trunk to be used, but have a functional snorkle that was bolted securely to the engine's intake?<BR> <BR>Now I feel like spending more money on parts for my Fiero . . . S H * T !||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||02-25-2001||11:12 AM||||Right  now my scoop is used to cool the engine compartment. I'm still deciding if I want to duct it into the engine. Due to the V-6 having the throttle facing left, it would require a special tube and air cleaner set-up. I don't know if the length and curves required would benefit from hooking to the scoop.  The only benefit I could see is cooler air. But it is amazing how much cooler the engine compartment is with the scoop.<BR> If you have a 4-cyl, the back edge of the scoop is right over the air cleaner on it. As for leaving it attached to the engine so the decklid could be opened, you would need to calculate the angle of the decklid when it was open and the angle of the scoop. I don't think you could do it without cutting a the decklid farther back. Mounting then becomes more difficult. They do have a hinge and latch method of attaching the scoop. It blocks the forward edge of the scoop but you could probably use it with the 4-cyl. The scoop lifts rearward then the decklid opens. Go to the link mentioned above by tpeerson and scroll to the bottom. You'll see a white GT with the decklid open and the scoop attached.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||02-25-2001||01:23 PM||||tpeerson--Unless you only plan on using the strobe for show, you might not want to mess with trying to get it hooked up.  Most states frown on colored, flashing lights of any color.  Just a thought.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000014||tpeerson||02-25-2001||08:28 PM||||&gt;&gt;Unless you only plan on using the strobe for show, you might not want to mess with trying to get it hooked up.&lt;&lt;<P>I figure it will be worth the most in working order, and I want to get top $ out of it. Whoever buys if can decide if they want to turn it on or not ;-). It would be a GREAT attention getter at shows, for the guy with an Indy who wants his car to look as close to the original pace car as possible.<BR>Or someone could tint the lens red, replace the strobes with regular bulbs and have a really cool 3rd brake light!<BR>BTW... New photos of the scoop primered are up at <A HREF="http://www.MoreThanMeetsTheEye.com/indy" TARGET=_blank>http://www.MoreThanMeetsTheEye.com/indy</A> <BR><P>------------------<BR>Tim Peerson<BR>87 V8 Testarossa rebody in progress<BR>http://www.MoreThanMeetsTheEye.com/vroom||||reg||1
